


	                          Pseudo-Steve
	                               By
	                        Stephen Willson

	                    Copyright (c) April 1980


	                            Overview


	       This paper describes the program "Pseudo-Steve",  which

	  I   believe   has   several   very  interesting  properties.

	  Pseudo-Steve  is  a  program  which  I  believe  models   my

	  organizational   style   to   some   extent.   It  has  been

	  pre-programmed with certain information about ICS  145.   It

	  is  capable  of  accessing this information in a logical and

	  consistent manner despite the natural language form  of  the

	  input questions.


	       I claim that everything in life is  context  sensitive.

	  This  program  uses  the  context  of a conversation to help

	  retrieve information and to resolve the meanings  of  words.

	  I  also  claim  that  it is useful to view the communication

	  process as the process of instructing another person/machine

	  to  search for information or to store some information in a

	  useful "place".


	       The major data structure used by this  program  is  the

	  tree.


	                Sample Session with Pseudo-Steve

	  
	  [Hello, I'm Pseudo-Steve.  Type HELP for help.]
	  
	  [Yes?] when can i talk to steve
	   During Steve's office hours, or by appointment.
	  
	                                                        Page 2


	  [Yes?] when are the hours
	   During Steve's office hours, or by appointment.
	  
	  [Yes?] when are the office hours
	   Steve's office hours are Mondays from 3-4 and Thursdays from 7-8 pm.
	  
	  [Yes?] what about kens
	   Ken's office hours are Tuesdays and Thurdays from 1:30-2:00.
	  
	  [Yes?] where
	   Ken's office is in room CS 245.
	  
	  [Yes?] when is assignment two due
	   Assignment two will be due April 24th at the beginning of class.
	  
	  [Yes?] when is it available
	   Assignment two will be available April 10th.
	  
	  [Yes?] whats for dinner
	  [I didn't understand the question, sorry.]
	  
	  [Yes?] how about assignment one
	  [I'm not sure I understood.]
	   Please be more specific about assignment one.
	  
	  [Yes?] when is it due
	   Assignment one will be due April 15th at the beginning of class.
	  
	  [Yes?] thanks
	   [You're welcome!]
	  

	                    The Tree and It's Import


	       This system uses a  hierarchically  organized  tree  to

	  store  the information.  The exact information stored in the

	  tree is, I believe currently,  rather  arbitrary.   But  the

	  nature  and  order that information is stored in the tree is

	  crucial.  Here is a part of the tree that Pseudo-Steve uses:

	  Steve-stuff
	          Steves-office
	                  Steves-office-hours
	                          Steves-office-hours-time
	                          Steves-office-hours-location
	                  Steves-office-location
	          Steves-extension

	  Each level of indentation indicates a  level  of  increasing

	                                                        Page 3


	  detail  about  a topic.  The arrangement above is useful for

	  parsing English, but other  structures  can  be  envisioned.

	  Perhaps this one:

	  Steve-stuff
	          Steves-office-hours
	                  Steves-office
	                  Steves-office-hours-time
	          Steves-extension

	  The reason I use the  first  tree  and  not  the  second  is

	  because  I hold my office hours in a different location than

	  my office, and the system needs to be able  to  discriminate

	  between these two possiblities.


	       Getting from English to a Destination in the Tree


	       The user types in an input question.  Instead of  using

	  the  strict  word order of the sentence, the system looks to

	  see if there is any information in the  sentence  that  will

	  allow  it  to  disambiguate  between any of several possible

	  higher-level subjects of discussion.  Some of  the  subjects

	  Pseudo-Steve  can  answer questions about are the class, the

	  assignments, the TA (me), or the instructor  (Ken).   If  no

	  choice  can  be made (probably because the question that was

	  input assumes some context) then the system  looks  back  to

	  see  what the topic of discussion in the last statement was,

	  and uses this information to pick a topic.   For  the  input

	  question,

	          "When are Steves office hours" 

	  the  system  decides  that the main topic of the question is

	  Steve-stuff.  Once the system has chosen the main  topic  of

	                                                        Page 4


	  the  question,  it focuses the search for the correct answer

	  to the information stored in the  tree  "under"  the  parent

	  node, in this case, "Steve-stuff".


	       This process continues.  Under Steve-stuff, the  system

	  wants  to  know if the question is about Steves-extension or

	  Steves-office.  The key word  office  indicates  the  search

	  should continue down the subtree that has information stored

	  about Steves-office.  And so on.


	                    Using a Previous Context


	       Suppose the next input question is "Where".  The system

	  once  again  starts  at the top of the tree and looks for an

	  indication in the input  question  about  which  subtree  to

	  search.   Finding  none,  it uses the path used last time to

	  indicate where to search.   In  this  case,  Steve-stuff  is

	  used.  The system continues to use this information whenever

	  the input question is not helpful.  In this particular case,

	  almost all the information comes from the previous context.


	       Suppose the next input question is "What  about  Kens".

	  The  system  uses  the  word  "Kens"  to  indicate  that the

	  Ken-stuff  subtree  should  be  searched.   After  that,  no

	  information  can  be used from the input sentence so all the

	  search indicators are taken from the  context  of  the  last

	  input  question.  The system naturally assumes that the user

	  wishes to know about Ken's office hours location.

	                                                        Page 5


	       Let's look at  an  example  that  seems  surprising  at

	  first.  Take the following dialog:

	          When is Steve available?
	          Steve is available during his office hours or by appointment.
	          What about assignment two?
	          Assignment two is a syntax analyzer.

	  The first question  sets  up  the  context  of  interest  in

	  availablity.   It  would  seem  reasonable  that  the second

	  question might refer to when assignment  two  is  available.

	  The  reason  the  system  doesn't  answer  that question and

	  instead answers what assignment two is about is because  the

	  system  was  able  to  guide  it's  search  for the required

	  information because of the keyword,  "What",  so  it  didn't

	  bother  to answer what the person might of meant.  But then,

	  this is the type of mistake that people make all  the  time.

	  The user could still respecify the search pattern by saying,

	  "But when is it due",  also  a  perfectly  natural  sounding

	  dialog, and the system will produce the correct answer.


	                  Why the System is not an ATN


	       My understanding of ATN's is that  the  input  text  is

	  scanned  in  a  left  to  right  manner,  guiding the system

	  through a state machine.  This process is clearly not a part

	  of  my  system.  Also, as the system moves through the state

	  machine it executes code to remember interesting facts about

	  the   input   sentence.    My   system   has  none  of  this

	  augmentation.


	                                                        Page 6


	                     What about Primitives?


	       Nobody wants to spend the next one hundred years typing

	  in very specific tree structures to a program like this one,

	  to enable it to discriminate fine shades  of  meaning  where

	  that  is  not necessary.  There are trees in campus park.  I

	  can visualize any one of the group of trees over by  Bio-Sci

	  without  any trouble.  I can visualize any one of the leaves

	  from that tree.  In fact, I have no idea what  those  leaves

	  REALLY look like, but I know what tree leaves look like, and

	  since I have no reason to doubt my experience, I figure that

	  the  leaves  COULD look like all the other leaves I've seen,

	  so that's what I visualize.


	       Another  example:   Visualize  a  phone  book,   opened

	  randomly  to the middle.  Now tell me the fifth phone number

	  in the left column of  the  right  hand  page,  and  who  it

	  belongs   to.    It   seems  people  are  quite  capable  of

	  visualizing a page from a phone book,  complete  with  phone

	  numbers, but when they try to look closely and see just what

	  it  is  they  have  visualized,  they  can't,  unless   they

	  construct  something from their past experience.  One of the

	  phone numbers COULD be my own, and I COULD recite it as  the

	  answer to the question, but we all know that would be BS.


	       What is the point?  The first example shows  that  even

	  though  I know about trees in general, it is not necessarily

	  true that I know ANYTHING about the leaves on the  trees  in

	  campus  park.   Therefore,  in  this  case,  "leaves"  is  a

	                                                        Page 7


	  primitive.   The  second  example   illustrates   the   same

	  principle.   "Phone  numbers" is a primitive.  You can't get

	  anymore specific about phone numbers in this example.


	              When is a Primitive not a Primitive?


	       If I am a collector of modern art,  then  when  talking

	  with  my  low-brow friends who know nothing of modern art, I

	  can point to a painting and say to them, there is  a  modern

	  art  style  painting.   If  I  am  talking  to my fellow art

	  collectors, and I point to a painting and say to them, there

	  is  a  modern  art  style  painting,  we will probably start

	  discussing in more detail it's style in terms more  specific

	  than simply, "that's modern art".


	       In the first case, the category  heading  "modern  art"

	  might  as  well  be  a primitive, but in the second case the

	  category heading "modern  art"  is  used  only  to  set  the

	  context for what's to come, conversationally.


	       Whats the point?  A primitive can be called a primitive

	  only if it's at a leaf of the knowledge tree.  It is clearly

	  possible to extend the knowledge tree below  this  level  if

	  necessary,  using  information from other parts of the tree.

	  But then the so-called primitive  loses  it's  status  as  a

	  primitive.


	       We've seen this happen with  CD.   Take  the  primitive

	  PTRANS.   Assume the case that corresponds to "Jack PTRANSed

	  himself to the airport".

	                                                        Page 8


	          (PTRANS (actor Jack)
	                  (object Jack)
	                  (from ?)
	                  (to airport))

	  Such a tree  structure  indicates  that  Jack  went  to  the

	  airport alright, but it doesn't specifiy enough information.

	  Actually, it is much more informative as a category  heading

	  about types of movement, though not necessarily one that can

	  be used to parse English usefully.   Scripts,  plans,  goals

	  and so on are also category headings.  If anything, a script

	  is merely a short-hand for a zillion CD "primitives".  Goals

	  are category headings for types of human behavior.


	       So,  the  point  is:   forget   about   searching   for

	  primitives.


	                 It's the Structure that's Hard


	       Every program I have had experience with is capable  of

	  searching  some  pre-defined knowledge structure.  It is the

	  same with Pseudo-Steve.  When coding up the system, I had to

	  be  very careful about the structure I imposed on the facts,

	  in order for the system to work.  All  the  intelligence  my

	  system  demonstrates (or doesn't) is due directly to the job

	  I did building the  knowledge  tree.   I  want  to  write  a

	  program  that  can  build these trees in a useful manner.  I

	  have a vague notion how to do this, but that's  for  another

	  time.

	                                                        Page 9


	       If such a program  could  be  written,  than  it  could

	  accept  a  story  and then answer questions about the story.

	  Since it is easy to answer questions about a  topic  already

	  understood,  we  can  guess  that the trick is to encode the

	  information so that it  can  be  most  easily  and  usefully

	  retrieved.


	                  What Good is English Anyway?


	       If our goal is  to  talk  to  the  machine  in  natural

	  language,  then  we might consider the fact that people have

	  been using English for many many years to specify  to  their

	  fellow     men    descriptions    of    problems,    events,

	  emotions--everything that people can  express.   It  is  not

	  unreasonable  to guess that if it is true that language is a

	  set of commands to search or modify the knowledge tree, then

	  the  language  that  people already use is the one we should

	  use for our machine.  I think that the  information  in  the

	  machine  should  be organized to make this process feasible,

	  and I have tried to model that in Pseudo-Steve.  Because  of

	  the context sensitivity, it is possible to store very subtle

	  meanings based on the  different  words  that  are  used  to

	  convey a thought.


	           Other Information is Implicit in the Tree


	       When given the question, "whats for dinner", the system

	  replies  that  it  didn't  understand  the question.  If the

	  system  simply  followed  key  words   without   using   the

	                                                       Page 10


	  hierarchic  structure, then it might well answer "compiler".

	  (It did once, in a very early version.) But it  is  possible

	  to  use  the  structure  of  the tree to figure out that the

	  system doesn't now the answer to the question.  How?  If the

	  system gets to a node in the tree that has sub-branches, but

	  no possible path down the tree is indicated,  and  there  is

	  unused  information  in  the input question, then the system

	  doesn't know the answer to whatever question was asked.   In

	  "whats  for  dinner", the system can't make any progress, so

	  it can say "I didn't understand the question or I don't know

	  the  answer".   This  models  the  fact that people can very

	  quickly determine what they don't know.


	                            Summary


	       It has been my experience that I arrage  informatin  in

	  my mind in a hierarchic "knowledge tree", and that I use the

	  context of an utterance to resolve the meaning of a word.  I

	  have  written  and  demonstrated  a program that models that

	  behavior.  Lastly, I claim that every event and  thought  in

	  life  is  context sensitive and that if we wish to model the

	  human mind we can and must model this  aspect  of  life.   I

	  think  my program shows that contextual knowledge eliminates

	  many otherwise  mind-boggling  problems  with  understanding

	  natural language.

